# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
policy_module(ai_tools_apphost, 0.1.0)

## ai_tools OPTIONAL GROUP: apphost  [DEFAULT: DISABLED, EXPERIMENTAL]
##
## Grants ai_tools_t map + execute on tmpfs (memfd) files, so the .NET host may
## execute code from an anonymous in-memory file. .NET creates an executable
## `apphost` and runs its W^X JIT by writing generated native code to a
## memfd_create(2) file and mapping it a second time PROT_EXEC. The core module
## grants `execmem` (anonymous read-write-execute pages, shared with V8's JIT) but
## NOT `execute` on a tmpfs FILE mapping, so that second mapping fails EACCES and any
## project that builds or runs a native host aborts:
##   - executable output types: console apps, ASP.NET Core, worker/hosted services
##   - test runners with an out-of-process host: xunit.v3, single-file publish
## Library builds and in-process test runners (MSTest on Microsoft.Testing.Platform)
## do NOT need it, which is why it is a separate group from tmpmap rather than folded
## into the dotnet integration or the base.
##
## SECURITY: this permits fileless in-memory execution (memfd + PROT_EXEC, the
## classic evasion primitive), but it does not grant a new PRIVILEGE. The domain already
## holds `execmem`, so in-process arbitrary native execution is already possible;
## this only extends it to a file-backed executable mapping. Code executed this way
## still runs as ai_tools_t -- there is no entrypoint from this domain to a more
## privileged one -- so an in-memory payload lands back in the same DAC + type
## confinement, with the handback boundary and no-sudo posture intact. It is off by
## default because it widens the surface (and detectability) around tmpfs execution.
##
## SCOPE: this is `execute` on TMPFS (memfd), which is distinct from the tmpmap group.
## tmpmap adds `map` on ai_tools_tmp_t (on-disk /tmp), never `execute`; /tmp stays
## mounted noexec, which no rule here changes. This grant does NOT touch ai_tools_tmp_t.
## It is NOT `execmod`: .NET's W^X JIT uses two separate mappings (one writable, one
## executable) of the same memfd, so it needs { map execute }, never a writable-and-
## executable single mapping.
##
## SECCOMP: memfd_create passes the session's @system-service SystemCallFilter (only
## namespace syscalls are filtered), so SELinux is the operative gate for this path.
##
## EXPERIMENTAL: the permission set below is the bring-up hypothesis. Verify it under
## permissive against a real executable build/run (selinux/avc) and trim to the
## observed minimum before promoting to stable; the graduation step is scoping the
## grant to a private memfd type via a tmpfs type_transition rather than the shared
## tmpfs_t.
##
## Enable:  sudo selinux/install-selinux.sh enable-group apphost
## Disable: sudo selinux/install-selinux.sh disable-group apphost
##
## When a task needs this group and it is not loaded, surface to the user:
##   "dotnet run / ASP.NET Core / xunit.v3 requires the SELinux 'apphost' policy group.
##    ! sudo selinux/install-selinux.sh enable-group apphost
##    Why: .NET executes its apphost and JIT'd code from an anonymous memfd file;
##         ai_tools_t has execmem but not execute on a tmpfs file mapping, so the
##         mapping fails EACCES. This adds map+execute on tmpfs (memfd) files."

require {
    type ai_tools_t;
    type tmpfs_t;
}

# memfd double-mapped W^X JIT / apphost: size and write the generated code through a
# read-write mapping of an anonymous tmpfs (memfd) file, then execute it from a second
# read-only mapping. The executable mapping needs `execute`; both mappings need `map`;
# creating, sizing and writing the memfd needs open/read/write/getattr. Anonymous memfd
# files created by ai_tools_t are labelled tmpfs_t.
allow ai_tools_t tmpfs_t:file { open read write getattr map execute };
